palOS login (paladin-login)

paladin-login is palOS's login manager. It starts the X server, shows the palOS login screen
(paladin-greeter, a Kibble app run as the unprivileged "paladin-login" account), checks passwords
through PAM (/etc/pam.d/paladin-login), and starts the chosen session registered with systemd-logind.
When the session ends, the X server starts afresh and the login screen comes back.

Settings: /etc/paladin-login.conf, then /etc/paladin-login.d/NAME.conf in name order:
  autologin-user, autologin-session   log someone in once when paladin-login starts
  default-session                     the session used when none is chosen (paladin)
  sessions-dirs                       where sessions are found
  display-setup-script, session-setup-script

The login screen's own look: /etc/paladin-greeter.conf. Each person's look on the login screen
(Dark Mode, desktop picture) is in /var/lib/paladin-login/users/NAME, written by their session.
On the palOS install USB the live account logs in automatically. The screen lock is palOS's own
(paladin-locker), not the login screen.
